  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/assets/1023ca76971805ffc07c0397565723973a6a65359cade8d0bfbfa5a5b63f8a5c.png)\n\nGiven that the points $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(6,5)$ and $(-6,-5),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?",
  "worked_solution": "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{\u2015}{AB}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{{(5-(-5))}^{2}+{(6-(-6))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{244} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 61} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{61}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\u2015}{AB}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{AB}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{61}}{2}=\\sqrt{61}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{61})^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
